dy":"This doesn't look right at first. If the type is generic, the name includes the generic type arguments; otherwise, we're referring to a different type altogether (`MyType` and `MyType<T>` are 2 different types).\r\n\r\nIn this particular case, the type symbol returns `true` when the containing type is generic despite the nested type not having generic type arguments itself. The nested type can be considered generic as well since it can use the generic type arguments of the containing type (`MyContainingType<int>.MyType` and `MyContainingType<float>.MyType` are 2 different types).\r\n\r\nI'm a bit worried that we may accidentally use this method with an unbound generic type and end up declaring an entirely new class, but it's probably fine as long as we are careful.\r\n","created_at":"2025-03-20T18:38:51Z","updated_at":"2025-03-20T18:52:07Z","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/104279#discussion_r2006238217","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/pulls/104279","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/pulls/comments/2006238217"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/104279#discussion_r2006238217"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/pulls/104279"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/pulls/comments/2006238217/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":186,"original_line":186,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":5,"position":5,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/pulls/comments/2007875583","pull_request_review_id":2706459743,"id":2007875583,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOAO6SJc53rb__","diff_hunk":"@@ -183,7 +183,7 @@ public static string GetAccessibilityKeyword(this INamedTypeSymbol namedTypeSymb\n \n         public 